  "success": true,
  "output": "I did a deep pass through the server layer (`lib/server/*`), the DB layer, the query/polling client code, and every stateful/animated component. Bottom line up front: **this codebase is unusually disciplined already** — the team clearly optimized for N+1s and leaks on purpose (see the comments in `conflicts.ts`, `entities.ts`, `export.ts` explicitly calling out \"one grouped query, not a query per row\"). So most of the \"obvious\" anti-patterns aren't present. What follows are the real, specific opportunities I found.\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns — none found, but one thing to protect\n\nEvery list screen already batches correctly:\n- `getQueuePage()` (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:172`) does rows + counts + child-counts as `Promise.all` + one grouped follow-up query, not per-row queries.\n- `countChildrenByParent()` (`lib/server/entities.ts:28`) takes an array of ids and does one `groupBy`, called once per page rather than once per row.\n- `buildRunExport()` (`lib/server/export.ts:122`) parallelizes the three sections it needs.\n\n**Nothing to fix here**, but it's worth adding a lint/review rule (or a comment convention, which they already half-have) so a future PR that adds a per-row `getDb().select()...` inside a `.map()` gets caught in review — there's no automated guard against regressing this today.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders in React\n\nThis is where the real findings are, because there is **zero `React.memo` usage anywhere in `components/`**, and several client components carry up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100` rows (`types/queue.ts:134`).\n\n**Finding A — every queue row re-renders on every keystroke/selection change**\n\n`ConflictQueue` (`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:34`) holds `selectedIndex`/`overrideConflictId` state and passes rows straight through to `ConflictDenseTable` / `ConflictFocus` / the ledger `.map`. Pressing `j` changes `selectedIndex`, which re-renders `ConflictQueue`, which re-renders **all 100 unmemoized row components**, even though visually only the previously-selected and newly-selected rows change.\n\n```tsx\n// components/conflicts/conflict-dense-table.tsx\nexport const ConflictDenseTable = memo(function ConflictDenseTable({ ... }) { ... })\n\n// and per-row, extract a Row component and memo it:\nconst Row = memo(function Row({ row, expanded, onSelect, ... }: RowProps) {\n  ...\n});\n```\n\nTo make `memo` actually pay off, the inline closures passed per-row need to stop being recreated every render:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx — today: submitFor(row.conflictId) is called inline in the\n// row's JSX, creating a new function per row per render.\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit => (action) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n// ...\nsubmit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}  // new closure every render\n```\n\nSince `ReviewActions`/`ConflictDiffRow` take a `conflictId` prop already in scope elsewhere, prefer passing `submit` (the stable top-level function from `useReview`) plus `conflictId`, and let the leaf call `submit(conflictId, action)` itself — that avoids allocating a closure per row per render entirely, and makes `memo` on the row effective.\n\n**Finding B — `RulesetEditor` re-sorts and re-scans on every keystroke**\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61`:\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);   // sorts the whole array, unmemoized\n...\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);             // O(n) lookup, inside a loop → O(n²)\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      key={rule.id}\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}        // another O(n) lookup, same loop\n      ...\n```\n\nTyping a single character in any field's `<Input>` re-runs the sort and both `indexOf` scans across the whole ruleset. With small rulesets this is cheap in absolute terms, but it's pure waste — the sort only needs to happen when `rules` changes, not on every render triggered by unrelated state (`message`, `saving`):\n\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst indexById = useMemo(\n  () => new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i])),\n  [rules],\n);\n...\n{ordered.map((rule, position) => (\n  <RuleRow key={rule.id} position={position + 1}\n    onChange={(patch) => update(indexById.get(rule.id)!, patch)} ... />\n))}\n```\n\n**Finding C — `TextType` recomputes `widest` every animation tick**\n\n`components/visual/text-type.tsx:101`: `phrases.reduce(...)` to find the longest phrase runs on **every render**, and this component re-renders on every character typed/deleted (every 28–62ms while animating). `phrases` is static for the component's lifetime, so this is pure waste on a hot path:\n\n```tsx\nconst widest = useMemo(\n  () => phrases.reduce((a, b) => (b.length > a.length ? b : a), \"\"),\n  [phrases],\n);\n```\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**Rulesets are immutable once saved** (the code says so explicitly — \"No in-place edit and no delete-then-save... v3 stays readable forever,\" `ruleset-editor.tsx:47`), yet `getRuleset(run.rulesetId)` is refetched from Postgres on every queue page load (`lib/server/conflicts.ts:226`). Same for `getRunDetail()` once a run is terminal (`status: \"complete\" | \"failed\"`) — a finished run's row never changes again except audit rows pointing at it.\n\nThese are ideal candidates for React's `cache()` (per-request dedupe is already implicit, but cross-request caching isn't happening) or `unstable_cache` keyed by id, since the underlying data is provably append-only/versioned:\n\n```ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const getRuleset = unstable_cache(\n  async (rulesetId: string) => { /* existing query */ },\n  [\"ruleset\"],\n  { revalidate: false }, // versioned rows never change once written\n);\n```\n\nFor `getRunDetail`, you'd need a conditional: cache only once `status` is terminal (a `queued`/`running` run's `stats` do change). A simple guard — cache the terminal case, bypass cache while in-flight — would cut a real chunk of DB round trips on the audit/export/conflicts screens, which all re-fetch the same finished-run metadata repeatedly.\n\n**Minor**: `QueryProvider` sets `staleTime: 0` (`components/query-provider.tsx:25`) with a comment explaining why (single-tab, interval already covers it) — that's a deliberate, correct choice, not a bug. No change needed there.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\nI checked every `useEffect`/`setInterval`/`requestAnimationFrame`/`ResizeObserver`/event-listener site (`RunLive`'s poll + clock, `TextType`'s timer, `LogoLoop`'s `ResizeObserver`, `GradientWaves`' WebGL render loop + context-lost handling). **All of them clean up correctly** — every effect returns a matching teardown (`clearInterval`, `cancelAnimationFrame`, `observer.disconnect()`, `removeEventListener`, and `GradientWaves` even tears down the GL context and calls `WEBGL_lose_context`). This is genuinely better hygiene than most codebases I review — nothing to fix here.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\n- `orderRulesByEvaluation` + double `indexOf` in `RulesetEditor` — covered above (#2B), it's both a re-render *and* a redundant-computation issue.\n- `TextType`'s `widest` — covered above (#2C).\n- `diffSegments()` (`lib/diff-chars.ts:32`) runs an O(n) prefix/suffix scan per cell per render in `DiffValue`. Given field values are short (account names/ids), this is not worth optimizing — flagging only so it's not mistaken for an oversight; leave as-is.\n\n## Summary table\n\n| Area | Verdict | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| N+1 queries | Clean | None — just guard against regression |\n| Re-renders | 3 real findings | Memo the queue rows + stabilize row-level callbacks; memoize `RulesetEditor`'s sort/index; memoize `TextType`'s `widest` |\n| Caching | 1 real opportunity | Cache immutable rulesets / terminal-run details across requests |\n| Memory leaks | Clean | None found |\n| Redundant computation | 2 real findings | Same as the `RulesetEditor` and `TextType` items above |\n\nThe highest-value fix is **Finding A** (memoizing queue rows) — it's the screen reviewers spend the most time in (\"a reviewer working a queue of 400 should never touch the mouse\"), and it's the one place where an unmemoized re-render actually scales with page size (up to 100 rows per keystroke).\n",
